:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Can rely on Rule 6c-11 (subject to conditions):**\n- Open-end index and fully transparent active ETFs, with daily holdings disclosure, written basket policies and website disclosures.\n- Leveraged/inverse ETFs. The 2020 Rule 18f-4 release removed their exclusion and rescinded their orders, so they must comply with 18f-4. That requires a derivatives risk management program and a relative VaR limit (200% of a reference portfolio), which effectively caps new funds near 2x. Pre-October 2020 funds above 2x were grandfathered. SEC staff letters in late 2025 reaffirmed the cap against 3x–5x filings.\n\n**Need separate exemptive relief:**\n- UIT ETFs.\n- Non-transparent or semi-transparent active ETFs, which need their own orders.\n- ETF share classes of multi-class funds. Dimensional's 2025 order was first, and others followed.\n\n**Outside the 1940 Act:**\n- Commodity and crypto trusts, registered under the '33 Act and listed under exchange rules, including the 2025 generic standards.\n\nMy knowledge runs to June 2026, so verify any later changes.","ttft_ms":5662,"type":"result","duration_ms":8281,"uuid":"6f20960a-2764-483b-81cb-5a974e97e6b7","ttft_stream_ms":1129,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":1129,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
